    { heading: "How to use a verse page to memorize Scripture", body: "Read the whole verse together first. Then let your child color one word at a time while saying the words already colored, so the verse gets longer as the page fills up. Short verses like Psalm 23:1 fit in one sitting for a five-year-old; longer ones like John 3:16 work better spread over a few days, a line at a time. When the page is done, hang it where your child will see it at breakfast and ask them to “read” it to you from the colors." },
    { heading: "Which verse to start with", body: "For the youngest children, Psalm 23:1 (“The LORD is my shepherd”) is the shortest and has the clearest picture. Joshua 1:9 is a good one before something new and scary, like a first day of school. The fruit of the Spirit page doubles as a list to talk through: pick one fruit, like patience or kindness, and look for it during the day. Matthew 19:14, Jesus welcoming the little children, is the one children tend to like best, because it is about them." },
